  {
   "publisher": "Elsevier BV",
   "abstract": "<jats:p>Vaccine hesitancy has risen in the United States, leading to declines in childhood immunization rates and increases in vaccine-preventable diseases. This paper examines whether direct-to-consumer (DTC) pharmaceutical advertising can mitigate vaccine hesitancy and increase uptake. Using commercial health insurance records and detailed advertising data, we analyze the impact of television advertising on the uptake of HPV vaccinations. We document a substantial level of advertising effectiveness in households with low vaccine hesitancy, and a more muted yet still positive impact among households with high vaccine hesitancy. These results point to the untapped potential of advertising as a policy tool to increase vaccine adoption. Ancillary analyses of ad content also show that mentions of side effects are associated with reduced ad effectiveness, though this negative impact is substantially larger among households with low levels of vaccine hesitancy compared to those with high vaccine hesitancy. This finding highlights the potential implications of policy requirements on households with different attitudes and the need to evaluate targeted messaging in the context of vaccine hesitancy.</jats:p>",
   "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.5877122",
